Class Activity 2: List 20 unix-based commands(which can also be said to be git commands) and their uses. 

ANS: 1. git init: initialize a new git repository.
2. git clone: clone a repository into a new directory
3. git add: add changes in your working directory to the staging area.
4. git status: show the status of changes as untracked.modified or staged.
5. git commit: record changes from the staging area to th repository.
6. git pull: fetch changes from a remote repository and merge them into the branch.
7. git push: push changes to a remote repoitory.
8. git branch: list, create, or delete branches.
9. git checkout: switch branches or restore working tree files.
10. git merge: combine changes from different branches.
11. git log: show the commit log.
12. git diff:show changes between commits, commit and working tree, etc.
13: git reset: reset current head to a specified state.
14. git remote: manage set of tracked repository.
15. git fetch: download objects and refs from another repository.
16. git stash: stash changes in a dirty working directory away.
17. git tag: create, list, delete or verify a tag object.
18. git log: show the commit log.
19. git cherry-pick: apply changes introduced by some existing commits.
20. git remote add: add a remote repository.